leap71/PicoGKRuntime @ 0f26321c (26.2): bulk lattice authoring in the C ABI.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of PicoGK (not affiliated with or endorsed by LEAP 71), ships the same code as a
separate translation unit (src/pico-bulk.cpp) so its vendored runtime stays
unmodified. This file is that code shaped as a diff against upstream.

This is the C ABI half only. The C# half, Lattice.AddBeam/AddSphere collecting
into a flat float array and flushing once per lattice instead of one P/Invoke
per element, belongs to leap71/PicoGK, a different repository. It is described
here rather than diffed, for the maintainers to take or adapt.

Why: Lattice_AddBeam is the busiest ABI entry point in picovoxel. One fixture
(LEAP71_HelixHeatX) crosses it 1,197,460 times across 37 lattices; bulk
transfer turns that into 37 calls. Measured on an Apple M2 Pro, WebAssembly
build, 2026-07-26: binding cost 50.1 -> 39.2 ns per beam, lattice authoring
238.7 -> 150.8 ms (paired, 5 repeats each way). The C# binding has the same
shape of cost.

Wire format: 8 floats per beam, (x, y, z, radius) for each endpoint, so two
vec4 lanes at a 32-byte stride with no padding under std140 or std430. The same
buffer can go to a GPU upload without repacking. Round-cap flags travel in a
parallel uint32 array.

Elements still go through Lattice::AddBeam/AddSphere, which keeps the bounding
box update and the rule that a zero-length round-capped beam becomes a sphere
(PicoGKLattice.h:213-218).

Output values: unchanged. Same elements, same order; voxelization is
byte-identical in picovoxel's suite (test/lattice-polyline.test.ts).

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree,
alone or after picogkruntime-bulk-mesh-abi.patch.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

--- a/API/PicoGK.h
+++ b/API/PicoGK.h
@@ -201,6 +201,24 @@
                                                             float               fRadiusA,
                                                             float               fRadiusB,
                                                             bool                bRoundCap);
+
+// Bulk lattice authoring. pfBeams is a flat float array, 8 floats per beam:
+// (x0, y0, z0, r0, x1, y1, z1, r1) -- endpoint A then endpoint B, radius in the
+// w lane of each, so one beam is two vec4 lanes at a 32-byte stride and the same
+// buffer can be uploaded to a GPU unrepacked. pnRoundCap is one uint32 per beam
+// (0 = flat cone). pfSpheres is 4 floats per sphere, (x, y, z, radius) -- the
+// same lane shape as one beam endpoint. Both return the number appended.
+
+PICOGK_API int32_t          Lattice_AddBeams(               PKINSTANCE          hInstance,
+                                                            PKLATTICE           hThis,
+                                                            const float*        pfBeams,
+                                                            const uint32_t*     pnRoundCap,
+                                                            int32_t             nCount);
+
+PICOGK_API int32_t          Lattice_AddSpheres(             PKINSTANCE          hInstance,
+                                                            PKLATTICE           hThis,
+                                                            const float*        pfSpheres,
+                                                            int32_t             nCount);
 
